                                MORNING



   She read two. A boy in Lagos, fifteen, whose English was better than
mine: I thought it was only me. It is not only me. A man on a ferry in Ber‐
gen, all lowercase, no last name, who said he’d hummed one note for
forty years and last night somebody hummed back.
   “They’ve been listening alone,” said Sofía, quietly. “All of them. For
years. Nobody told them there was a room.”



By the second week the news came at me through the left ear only. The
app was pulled from the stores and its servers switched off. The found‐
ation was dissolved by a board that had suddenly remembered it had a
lawyer. The Calm Index was withdrawn from every school that had
ever used it, and six hundred and twelve families received by certified
mail the files they’d never been shown. Vale answered questions
through three lawyers and without a notebook he was no longer per‐
mitted to keep.
   Not everything went. The holding company above Aperture
changed its name, the way Sofía said it would. The speakers in King‐
ston and Lagos stayed up for months. The landlord on Creston was still
a monster, and rent was still rent. What stopped was the Bronx count.
Nobody in the borough was being written down in eights anymore,
and the forty from the ring started a group chat that was mostly argu‐
ments. The four closure orders were withdrawn before a judge ever
read them, and Dalia framed the June 14 report and hung it over her
desk.
   I wrote Danny a letter and rewrote it six times. He answered with
two lines: he’d started at a hearing clinic in Yonkers, and his right ear
was still bad. I kept both lines. Dr. Okafor got her recording, finally,
and listened to all of it twice before she said a word.
   I went for a haircut that week. Not because I needed one.
   Papo and Wilson were arguing.
   I could hear it from the sidewalk, on my good side, before I reached
the door. A fight, a real one, with volume and conviction and a refer‐
ence to a relief pitcher who had been dead since 1995.




